1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,080
Moving on to the last section of this chapter,

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:03,600
infectious disorders.

3
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,000
The first disorder is impetigo.

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,880
This is a superficial bacterial skin infection,

5
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:12,240
usually due to Staph aureus or strep pyogenes,

6
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:13,720
and it commonly infects children.

7
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:16,000
In fact, it's extremely contagious.

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,960
It presents as erythematous macules.

9
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:21,400
Remember that a macule is a flat lesion

10
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:22,840
that progressed to pustules.

11
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:25,440
A pustule is a raised lesion that contains pus.

12
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:27,000
Of course, there's bacteria here,

13
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,560
so you're gonna get some pus.

14
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:30,680
It usually occurs on the face.

15
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:32,520
It'll result in an erosion

16
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:36,880
with this dry, crusted, honey-colored serum on the surface,

17
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:39,640
and so that's the classic finding in impetigo.

18
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:43,640
Cellulitis is also due to Staph aureus or strep pyogenes.

19
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:45,800
However, instead, you get a deeper infection

20
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:47,120
within the dermis,

21
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:52,000
and this presents as a tender, red, swollen rash with fever.

22
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,000
The risk factors include recent surgery

23
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,200
that would introduce bacteria to the dermis,

24
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,120
trauma, which could introduce bacteria to the dermis,

25
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:01,240
or, for example, an insect bite,

26
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:04,480
which could also introduce bacteria to the dermis.

27
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:08,080
Now, this can then progress to necrotizing fasciitis,

28
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:10,800
where you get necrosis of subcutaneous tissue

29
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:12,960
underneath the dermis due to infection

30
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:15,880
with anaerobic flesh-eating bacteria.

31
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:17,920
Of course, these anaerobic flesh-eating bacteria

32
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:21,040
would produce CO2, and that would lead to crepitus,

33
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:23,320
where you could feel gas within the skin.

34
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:25,800
You could feel the bubbles of gas within skin,

35
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:28,480
and this is a surgical emergency.

36
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:30,800
Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome

37
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:33,440
is when you get a staph infection of the skin.

38
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:36,400
It's usually the very superficial layers of the skin.

39
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:40,240
That staph produces exfoliative A and B toxins,

40
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,640
which then results in the lysis of the skin

41
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:44,840
and a separation of the layers of the skin,

42
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:46,920
giving a scalded appearance.

43
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:49,840
And so what you're gonna get clinically is sloughing of the skin

44
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:51,640
with an erythematous rash.

45
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:53,640
It occurs diffusely across the skin,

46
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:55,920
giving you significant skin loss.

47
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:58,440
This is, again, due to a staph aureus infection.

48
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:01,600
It produces exfoliative A and B toxins

49
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:04,800
that then separate the stratum granulosum

50
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:06,480
from the rest of the skin.

51
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:09,160
Now, remember that the stratum granulosum

52
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:10,760
is way up at the top of the skin.

53
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:12,360
You've got your basal layer,

54
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:14,360
then you've got your stratum spinosum,

55
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:16,760
or the spinal layer with the desmosomes,

56
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:19,000
and then you've got your stratum granulosum,

57
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,360
or the granular layer.

58
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:23,600
And then finally, you've got your stratum corneum.

59
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:24,760
And so what's happening here

60
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:27,840
is that you're getting separation very superficially

61
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:31,000
at the layer of the stratum granulosum.

62
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,040
And it's important to note where this is occurring,

63
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:34,560
because you need to distinguish it

64
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,840
from toxic epidermal necrolysis,

65
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:40,360
where you also get massive sloughing off of the skin.

66
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:42,640
However, in toxic epidermal necrolysis,

67
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:45,480
you're separating at the dermal-epidermal junction,

68
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:48,200
and so it's a much deeper separation.

69
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:51,120
Veruca is a term that refers to warts.

70
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:55,120
This is a flesh-colored papule, or a raised lesion,

71
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:56,640
with a rough surface.

72
00:02:56,640 --> 00:02:59,760
It's due to HPV, and the hands and feet

73
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:01,920
are a particularly common location.

74
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:04,440
Here's a classic picture of a wart or a veruca,

75
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:06,200
and again, this is due to HPV.

76
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:08,040
You can see that it's got a rough surface,

77
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:10,960
and it's a raised papule.

78
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:13,560
The final lesion is molluscum contagiosum.

79
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:17,880
This refers to a firm pink umbilicated papule,

80
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:20,600
and it's due to pox virus.

81
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:22,720
This most often arises in children,

82
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:25,040
but it can occur in sexually active adults.

83
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:27,480
It can be sexually transmitted, actually.

84
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:31,120
And it can also arise in immunocompromised individuals.

85
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:32,680
Now the hallmark of this lesion

86
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:35,600
is that you're going to see molluscum bodies

87
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:37,800
within the affected keratinocyte.

88
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,960
Again, this is a sign that these keratinocytes

89
00:03:39,960 --> 00:03:41,880
are infected with pox virus.

90
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:44,360
And so here are the molluscum bodies

91
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:46,640
indicative of viral inclusions.

92
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:49,840
And again, the key virus, pox virus.

93
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:52,760
And that concludes both this section and the chapter.

